1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,065
Your build works. The jar sits on your laptop. And your teammate cannot use it.

2
00:00:05,615 --> 00:00:06,940
Until you publish it,

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,715
to the repository your whole company shares.

4
00:00:09,715 --> 00:00:11,418
That step is called deploy.

5
00:00:11,968 --> 00:00:14,348
Today you learn the two kinds of version,

6
00:00:14,348 --> 00:00:15,567
snapshot and release,

7
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:18,005
and the one rule that surprises everybody.

8
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:21,094
Then you publish, and break the rule on purpose.

9
00:00:21,644 --> 00:00:22,865
This is CloudCart,

10
00:00:22,865 --> 00:00:25,646
the app we build all through this course.

11
00:00:25,646 --> 00:00:27,817
Version zero is a Maven project.

12
00:00:27,817 --> 00:00:32,769
Today its cart core module becomes a package your teammates can download.

13
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:33,176
Later,

14
00:00:33,176 --> 00:00:34,872
in the nexus masterclass,

15
00:00:34,872 --> 00:00:36,907
you repeat this on real nexus,

16
00:00:36,907 --> 00:00:40,503
and CloudCart version one wires it into the pipeline.

17
00:00:41,053 --> 00:00:42,203
One honest note.

18
00:00:42,203 --> 00:00:47,382
The company repository in this lesson is a small local stand-in I wrote,

19
00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:49,396
with a login and a web page.

20
00:00:49,396 --> 00:00:54,071
The real thing is a repository manager like nexus or artifactory,

21
00:00:54,071 --> 00:00:56,733
but Maven uploads to it the same way.

22
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:02,063
Install copies your jar into your own dot m two folder.

23
00:01:02,063 --> 00:01:04,880
Deploy uploads it to a remote repository,

24
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,667
so others can download it.

25
00:01:07,217 --> 00:01:09,117
A repository manager usually has two repositories,

26
00:01:09,117 --> 00:01:10,599
one for snapshots and one for releases.

27
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:11,967
You create each one in its web page,

28
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:13,638
and choose its type and its redeploy policy.

29
00:01:14,188 --> 00:01:16,056
A snapshot is work in progress.

30
00:01:16,056 --> 00:01:18,224
The version ends with dash snapshot.

31
00:01:18,224 --> 00:01:20,332
You may publish it again and again.

32
00:01:20,882 --> 00:01:26,322
A release is final. The version has no snapshot. Once published, it can never be replaced.

33
00:01:26,872 --> 00:01:28,717
The pom says where to upload.

34
00:01:28,717 --> 00:01:31,071
In the block distribution management,

35
00:01:31,071 --> 00:01:32,726
one address for snapshots,

36
00:01:32,726 --> 00:01:34,062
and one for releases.

37
00:01:36,112 --> 00:01:39,931
Why can a release never be replaced? Because other people built on it.

38
00:01:40,481 --> 00:01:43,867
If the jar for one dot zero dot zero changed tomorrow,

39
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:46,062
an old build could not be repeated.

40
00:01:46,062 --> 00:01:48,758
The same version would mean different code.

41
00:01:49,308 --> 00:01:51,169
So the repository refuses.

42
00:01:51,169 --> 00:01:52,886
It answers four oh nine,

43
00:01:52,886 --> 00:01:53,531
conflict.

44
00:01:53,531 --> 00:01:56,107
The right fix is never to overwrite.

45
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:58,183
Publish one dot zero dot one.

46
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:00,365
A snapshot is different.

47
00:02:00,365 --> 00:02:01,997
Maven adds a time stamp,

48
00:02:01,997 --> 00:02:03,970
so each upload is a new file,

49
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:05,602
and the newest one wins.

50
00:02:06,152 --> 00:02:07,048
And the login?

51
00:02:07,048 --> 00:02:08,584
The same as last lesson.

52
00:02:08,584 --> 00:02:09,544
A server block,

53
00:02:09,544 --> 00:02:10,632
with the same id,

54
00:02:10,632 --> 00:02:13,576
and the password from an environment variable.

55
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:18,017
Start in the repository's web page.

56
00:02:18,017 --> 00:02:19,041
Open a browser,

57
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:20,476
and go to local host,

58
00:02:20,476 --> 00:02:22,047
port eighty-eighty-two,

59
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:22,730
slash U I.

60
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:29,549
It asks for a login. The user is dev, and the password is the one from our locked file.

61
00:02:31,599 --> 00:02:33,122
No repositories yet.

62
00:02:33,122 --> 00:02:35,709
A repository manager starts empty,

63
00:02:35,709 --> 00:02:38,754
and you create each repository yourself.

64
00:02:38,754 --> 00:02:40,581
Click create repository.

65
00:02:42,131 --> 00:02:44,178
First the snapshot repository.

66
00:02:44,178 --> 00:02:46,362
The name is corp dash snapshots.

67
00:02:46,362 --> 00:02:47,795
The type is snapshot.

68
00:02:47,795 --> 00:02:50,046
And the policy is allow redeploy,

69
00:02:50,046 --> 00:02:52,776
because a snapshot changes all the time.

70
00:02:56,326 --> 00:02:58,929
Create it. Then the same again, for releases.

71
00:03:02,479 --> 00:03:04,554
The name is corp dash releases.

72
00:03:04,554 --> 00:03:05,892
The type is release.

73
00:03:05,892 --> 00:03:08,235
And the policy is disable redeploy,

74
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:11,247
so a published release can never be replaced.

75
00:03:14,797 --> 00:03:16,013
Two repositories.

76
00:03:16,013 --> 00:03:17,801
Each has its own address,

77
00:03:17,801 --> 00:03:19,733
ending in slash repository,

78
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:20,806
slash its name.

79
00:03:20,806 --> 00:03:23,095
Maven needs these two addresses.

80
00:03:25,145 --> 00:03:28,303
Now the terminal. Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

81
00:03:28,853 --> 00:03:32,002
Look at the upload addresses in the pom.

82
00:03:32,002 --> 00:03:32,789
Type: sed,

83
00:03:32,789 --> 00:03:33,341
dash n,

84
00:03:33,341 --> 00:03:35,230
eleven comma twenty-two,

85
00:03:35,230 --> 00:03:35,388
p,

86
00:03:35,388 --> 00:03:36,490
pom dot X M L.

87
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:38,570
The same two addresses,

88
00:03:38,570 --> 00:03:40,100
snapshots and releases.

89
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:41,564
Both with the id corp,

90
00:03:41,564 --> 00:03:43,227
so one login serves both.

91
00:03:43,777 --> 00:03:44,866
Set the password from the locked file.

92
00:03:44,866 --> 00:03:45,927
Type: export REPO_PASS equals dollar,

93
00:03:45,927 --> 00:03:46,272
parenthesis,

94
00:03:46,272 --> 00:03:46,386
cat,

95
00:03:46,386 --> 00:03:46,558
tilde,

96
00:03:46,558 --> 00:03:46,730
slash,

97
00:03:46,730 --> 00:03:47,275
dot corp dash pass.

98
00:03:47,825 --> 00:03:53,564
Deploy the snapshot. Type: mvn dash B dash q deploy, then list the snapshot repository.

99
00:03:54,514 --> 00:03:56,327
Maven builds, then uploads.

100
00:04:03,877 --> 00:04:06,267
One jar, with a time stamp in its name.

101
00:04:06,817 --> 00:04:11,041
Deploy it again. The same command.

102
00:04:13,491 --> 00:04:14,685
A second upload.

103
00:04:21,235 --> 00:04:25,545
Two jars now. Each upload is a new file, and nothing was refused.

104
00:04:26,095 --> 00:04:31,748
Now make it a release. Remove the word snapshot from the version, with sed.

105
00:04:32,298 --> 00:04:35,626
Version one dot zero dot zero. No snapshot.

106
00:04:36,176 --> 00:04:40,336
Deploy it. Type: mvn dash B dash q deploy, then list the release repository.

107
00:04:42,786 --> 00:04:43,981
Uploading the release.

108
00:04:50,531 --> 00:04:51,939
The release is published.

109
00:04:52,489 --> 00:04:57,694
Now the rule. Deploy the same version again, and keep only the error.

110
00:04:58,244 --> 00:05:00,057
Same version, second time.

111
00:05:06,607 --> 00:05:07,486
Four oh nine.

112
00:05:07,486 --> 00:05:08,095
Conflict.

113
00:05:08,095 --> 00:05:10,530
The repository protects the release.

114
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:11,815
To change anything,

115
00:05:11,815 --> 00:05:13,370
you need a new version.

116
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:17,120
Back to the web page. Click browse, to see what arrived.

117
00:05:19,170 --> 00:05:23,309
The snapshot repository now holds two uploaded jars. Open it.

118
00:05:23,859 --> 00:05:24,842
One component,

119
00:05:24,842 --> 00:05:25,544
cart core,

120
00:05:25,544 --> 00:05:28,915
with the version one dot zero dot zero snapshot.

121
00:05:28,915 --> 00:05:30,109
Open the version.

122
00:05:32,159 --> 00:05:32,748
Two jars.

123
00:05:32,748 --> 00:05:34,712
Each has its own build number,

124
00:05:34,712 --> 00:05:35,432
time stamp,

125
00:05:35,432 --> 00:05:36,871
and shaw one checksum.

126
00:05:36,871 --> 00:05:38,246
Nothing was replaced.

127
00:05:38,246 --> 00:05:38,639
Below,

128
00:05:38,639 --> 00:05:43,743
the dependency block a teammate pastes into their own pom to use your package.

129
00:05:44,293 --> 00:05:50,352
Now the release repository. One jar, version one dot zero dot zero. Fixed forever.

130
00:05:52,402 --> 00:05:52,714
Last,

131
00:05:52,714 --> 00:05:53,711
the events page.

132
00:05:53,711 --> 00:05:55,145
Every upload is logged.

133
00:05:55,145 --> 00:05:57,514
The red lines are the refused uploads,

134
00:05:57,514 --> 00:05:58,324
four oh nine,

135
00:05:58,324 --> 00:05:59,758
each with an event I D.

136
00:05:59,758 --> 00:06:02,002
Your lab asks for one of those I Ds.

137
00:06:03,552 --> 00:06:04,429
Your lab task.

138
